  "narrative": "All twelve newly delivered discovery results support the four central accepted dataset associations with very large, precise effects in the specified technical or culture contexts. H68-R41 restricts SMARCA4-loss versus non-loss to high-quality screens (screen_roc_auc >= 0.9555) and gives a signed effect of +0.791, interval [0.764, 0.819], with adequate cells. H76-R45 restricts the same SMARCA4/SMARCA2 contrast to high-serum culture and gives +0.799, interval [0.774, 0.824]. H63-R50 restricts it to the largest batch, batch_a, and gives +0.747, interval [0.709, 0.785]. Thus H68, H76, and H63 are accepted and closed as dataset-level robustness confirmations. This argues that the SMARCA4-loss/SMARCA2-dependency association is not driven only by low-quality screens, high-serum media, or absence/presence of batch_a effects. It does not constitute independent validation, because these are subsets of the same discovery dataset.\n\nThe KEAP1-loss/NFE2L2-dependency association similarly remains robust. H69-R42 gives +0.793, interval [0.764, 0.822] in high-quality screens; H77-R46 gives +0.814, interval [0.788, 0.840] in high-serum culture; and H64-R51 gives +0.812, interval [0.765, 0.858] within batch_a. All are accepted and closed. Given the earlier Avana-library confirmation and independent validation for the Avana restriction, the overall H13 KEAP1/NFE2L2 association is now well supported within this dataset, though still not proven causal or externally generalizable.\n\nThe MSI-high/WRN-dependency association also survives these technical and context restrictions. H70-R43 gives +0.790, interval [0.751, 0.829] in high-quality screens; H78-R47 gives +0.784, interval [0.746, 0.823] in high-serum culture; and H65-R52 gives +0.824, interval [0.766, 0.882] within batch_a. The exposed MSI-high cell in batch_a is small (37) but meets the pre-specified minimum cell-size rule, and the interval remains far above the clinical relevance threshold. These are accepted and closed. The H10 overall MSI/WRN association therefore appears robust to screen quality, serum context, and major batch restriction, but it still lacks direct independent validation.\n\nThe TP53-wild-type versus TP53-loss contrast for USP7 dependency remains strong and robust. H71-R44 gives +0.426, interval [0.332, 0.519] in high-quality screens; H79-R48 gives +0.443, interval [0.353, 0.532] in high-serum culture; and H62-R49 gives +0.417, interval [0.284, 0.550] within batch_a. All are accepted and closed. Combined with the already validated overall USP7 association and the high-mutation-burden validation, this makes the USP7 result the best-supported main association in the current evidence set.\n\nThe main scientific concern is not statistical direction but residual confounding and generalization. These are discovery-sample restrictions, not independent validation. The consistency across high-quality screens, serum context, and batch_a reduces but does not eliminate explanations involving unmeasured co-alterations, ploidy, growth rate, batch interactions, library effects, or synthetic assay artifacts. I request independent validation for H9 (SMARCA4 loss versus non-loss for SMARCA2 dependency) because H9 is a central accepted main association that has not yet received independent validation, has good discovery cell sizes, and has survived multiple within-dataset robustness restrictions. This validation should help distinguish a reproducible dataset-level association from discovery-sample noise or correlated context artifacts. No causal mechanism or external generalizability is being claimed."
