  "narrative": "The new results substantially strengthen the central within-dataset associations, although they remain robustness subsets rather than external validation. For SMARCA4-loss versus SMARCA2 dependency, the non-Avana screens give a very large same-direction effect (R67: +0.801, CI 0.748 to 0.853), and Ky-library screens give +0.816 (CI 0.777 to 0.856); both support the already validated H9. For KEAP1 loss versus NFE2L2 dependency, non-Avana and Ky screens also reproduce the large weakened-dependency association (R68: +0.794, CI 0.746 to 0.842; R71: +0.794, CI 0.752 to 0.837), supporting H13 beyond the Avana-only subset. For MSI-high versus WRN dependency, exclusions of KEAP1-loss and MSI-stable/ARID1A states remain very strong where tested (R74: +0.787; R76: +0.798), consistent with the validated H10 effect being robust to some major co-alterations. For TP53 wild-type versus USP7 dependency, the co-alteration exclusion claims remain large and precise (R75 among MSI-stable +0.401; R78 among MYC-amplification-negative +0.376), supporting H15. However, the library-restricted USP7 results are more nuanced: non-Avana aggregate gives +0.321 but with a small comparator cell and a lower bound only +0.085 (R69), and Ky-library gives +0.212 (CI 0.051 to 0.373, R72). These are directionally positive and statistically non-null, but smaller than Avana effects, so I accept the non-Avana aggregate claim but keep it active for magnitude uncertainty, and leave H123 unresolved because the Ky estimate suggests possible guide-library attenuation. The co-alteration exclusions H116-H119, H126, and H128 all have tight intervals far from zero and are accepted/closed. Overall, the evidence continues to support SMARCA4 loss -> stronger SMARCA2 dependency, KEAP1 loss -> weaker NFE2L2 dependency, MSI-high -> stronger WRN dependency, and TP53 wild-type -> stronger USP7 dependency as dataset-level associations, with an unresolved caveat that USP7/TP53 effect size may be smaller in some library subsets. I request independent validation for H13 because the unrestricted KEAP1-loss/NFE2L2 association is central and, while strongly replicated across many subsets, has direct validation only for the Avana-restricted H36.",
