  "narrative": "The new validation responses materially strengthen the core within-dataset conclusions and resolve several context tests.\n\nH13/KEAP1 loss versus NFE2L2 dependency: validation R79 is a large signed positive contrast (+0.791; independent interval +0.763 to +0.820) under the +1 direction, meaning KEAP1-loss lines have markedly higher/less-negative NFE2L2 dependency scores than KEAP1-intact lines. Combined with discovery R8, this is a very strong supported association within this dataset: KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker NFE2L2 dependency. I accept H13 and close it.\n\nH14/ARID1A loss versus SMARCA2 dependency: validation R80 is essentially null (signed estimate -0.002; independent interval -0.062 to +0.058), and discovery R14 was also near null. These are far below the magnitude of the accepted core dependencies. I reject H14 and close it: ARID1A loss is not associated with a scientifically meaningful difference in SMARCA2 dependency in the registered direction.\n\nH158/TP53 wild-type versus TP53 loss in high screen_nnmd: validation R106 gives a precise positive signed effect (+0.386; independent interval +0.241 to +0.532), consistent with discovery R97. Because the claim is registered with direction -1 and exposed=0/comparator=1, the raw contrast is more negative for TP53 wild-type lines, i.e. stronger USP7 dependency in TP53 wild-type cells. This supports the USP7 association even in higher screen_nnmd subsets. I accept H158 and close it.\n\nH115/USP7 TP53 wild-type effect interaction with high mutation burden: discovery R65 was modestly positive signed (+0.133; interval crossing zero), but validation R107 is much smaller (+0.055) and the independent interval is wide (-0.231 to +0.342). This does not support a robust mutation-burden interaction strong enough to accept. The interval also leaves open the possibility of a moderate attenuation or enhancement, so I leave the scientific status unresolved rather than declaring a clean negative effect-modification conclusion, but I close the investigation because further work here is low priority relative to robustness checks and the main USP7 association remains well supported.\n\nCurrent supported within-dataset findings: SMARCA4 loss is associated with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency; MSI-high status is associated with much stronger WRN dependency; KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker NFE2L2 dependency; TP53 wild-type status is associated with stronger USP7 dependency. These are large, precise, repeatedly replicated, and independently validated for the central claims. They are associations in the supplied lung dataset, not causal claims or external-generalizable claims.\n\nSupported or strongly negative context findings: the core effects persist in high screen_nnmd, high/low screen ROC, Avana/non-Avana, Ky/non-Ky, high/low serum, adherent culture, batch_a, and many co-alteration subsets. Ananeuploidy interactions for SMARCA2, NFE2L2, and WRN are near null. RTK/MAPK pathway-bypass claims and many low-prior marker associations are near null and not supported. The Ky-library USP7 interaction remains non-robust: the Ky direct effect is positive but smaller than non-Ky, while the formal interaction did not replicate consistently.\n\nMost informative next analyses: I registered direct core-effect tests in the complement screen-quality subsets, specifically low screen_nnmd and low Cas9 activity score. If the four core associations remain large in low-screen-depth/low-Cas9-activity settings, screen-quality artifacts become much less plausible. If one or more attenuates, that would identify an important technical-context boundary. I also intend the analysis stage to prioritize any pending low-media, low-mutation-burden, and other direct robustness tests over further low-prior marker screening unless a strong signal emerges.",
  "research_notes": "Core accepted and validated within-dataset associations: SMARCA4 loss -> stronger SMARCA2 dependency; KEAP1 loss -> weaker NFE2L2 dependency; MSI-high -> stronger WRN dependency; TP53 wild-type -> stronger USP7 dependency. These are internal associations, not causal or externally generalizable.\n\nLatest validation decisions: H13 accepted/closed with R79; H14 rejected/closed with R80; H158 accepted/closed with R106; H115 unresolved/closed because validation R107 is near null and wide despite discovery R65 pointing modestly positive.\n\nPrevious context conclusions remain: Ky-library USP7 attenuation is not robust (H130 rejected; H143 unresolved/closed); aneuploidy interactions for SMARCA2/NFE2L2/WRN rejected; RTK/MAPK bypass hypotheses rejected; low-prior research markers are not useful.\n\nNext plan: prioritized registered direct robustness tests in low screen_nnmd and low cas9_activity_score subsets for the four core outcomes. Also keep low-media and low-TMB direct tests in mind if analyze selects them. Avoid additional low-prior marker screening unless a core association fails in a direct stress test. Remaining validation budget is reserved for future high-impact robustness failures or newly accepted high-impact subset claims."
