  "narrative": "The new results test the four core within-dataset associations in complementary screen-quality and biological-complement subsets: low screen_nnmd, low Cas9 activity, low media_serum_pct, and low mutation_burden. All 12 comparisons are valid by the cell-size rule and all signed estimates are positive in the registered direction. For the SMARCA2/smarca4_loss claims, positive signed estimates mean SMARCA4-loss cells have more negative dependency scores than SMARCA4-intact cells, i.e. stronger SMARCA2 dependency. For the WRN/msi_high claims, positive signed estimates mean MSI-high cells have stronger WRN dependency. For the USP7/tp53_loss claims, because exposure is TP53 wild-type and comparator is TP53 loss, positive signed estimates mean TP53 wild-type cells have stronger USP7 dependency. For the NFE2L2/keap1_loss claim, the +1 direction means KEAP1-loss cells have less negative NFE2L2 dependency scores, i.e. weaker NFE2L2 dependency.\n\nThe low screen_nnmd tests are highly supportive: H168/R108, H169/R109, and H170/R110 remain large (+0.81, +0.79, and +0.79) and precise for SMARCA2, NFE2L2, and WRN, and H171/R111 remains large for USP7 (+0.42). The low Cas9 tests are similarly supportive: H172/R112, H173/R113, and H174/R114 remain around +0.77 to +0.81 for SMARCA2, NFE2L2, and WRN, while H175/R115 remains +0.41 for USP7. The low media tests for NFE2L2, WRN, and USP7 are also positive: H148/R116 +0.77, H149/R117 +0.80, and H150/R118 +0.33. These results make screen-depth, Cas9-activity, and low-serum artifact explanations much less plausible for the core associations, because even in the lower-quality complement subsets the effects are far larger than the small null signals seen for low-prior markers.\n\nH61/R119, the USP7 association among low mutation_burden lines, is the smallest of the new accepted effects but still scientifically meaningful and precise (+0.315; 95% CI +0.212 to +0.417). It complements the independently validated high mutation_burden USP7 effect and suggests the USP7/TP53 wild-type association is not restricted to high mutation burden. Because H61 is a newly accepted boundary claim and the most modest new effect, I request independent validation for H61. If validation confirms the low mutation_burden USP7 effect, USP7 will be robust across the mutation-burden axis in this dataset; if validation attenuates, that would define a meaningful context boundary rather than discard the central USP7 association.\n\nMultiplicity remains a concern because many comparisons have been run, but these new results are not marginal: the intervals exclude zero and the magnitudes are much larger than both assay noise and the 10% relative-difference criterion. I therefore accept all 12 new comparisons as evidence for the registered within-dataset associations, close the subset replications except H61, and keep H61 active pending validation. These conclusions remain associations in the supplied lung dataset and do not establish causality or external generalizability.",
  "research_notes": "Current status: the four core within-dataset associations now have broad robustness support across high/low screen_nnmd, high/low screen ROC, high/low Cas9 activity, high/low media serum, high/low mutation burden, library subsets, lineage/adherent culture/co-alteration complements, and batch_a. SMARCA4 loss -> stronger SMARCA2 dependency and KEAP1 loss -> weaker NFE2L2 dependency are especially stable. MSI-high -> stronger WRN dependency is stable. TP53 wild-type -> stronger USP7 dependency is stable but smaller; low-mutation-burden effect accepted but newly pending validation. Low-prior marker/RTK-bypass associations remain near null and should not be expanded. Next priority: assess H61 validation. If H61 validates, USP7 can be treated as robust across TMB; if not, limit USP7 claims to higher TMB or central overall association and keep H83/context tests informative. Avoid further broad marker screening; focus remaining analyses on high-utility specificity/interaction tests already registered, e.g. USP7 TMB interaction, Cas9/media interactions, MYC/WRN in MSI-negative lines, CDKN2A specificity for SMARCA2, and RTK cross-dependency hypotheses only if prior signal emerges.",
