  "narrative": "The newly available validation evidence resolves two pending robustness questions and closes one non-robust interaction. For H158, the independent validation estimate for the TP53-wild-type effect on dependency_USP7 in the screen_nnmd stratum is +0.386 (95% interval +0.240 to +0.532), closely matching discovery (+0.370). Because the claim direction is -1, this signed positive estimate means that TP53-wild-type cells have stronger USP7 dependency than TP53-loss cells within this screen-quality stratum. The interval excludes zero and is large relative to the null signals elsewhere in the dataset, so I keep H158 accepted and close the investigation. For H186, the independent validation estimate is +0.760 (95% interval +0.674 to +0.847), confirming the very large discovery estimate +0.833. Under the registered direction, positive signed estimates mean MSI-high cells have much stronger WRN dependency than MSI-negative cells among TP53-loss lines. Although the exposed MSI-high/TP53-loss cell is small, the effect is far larger than the uncertainty and robust in an independent validation, so I accept H186 and close it. For H115, the MYC-amplification interaction claim remains non-robust: discovery gave +0.134 (95% -0.048 to +0.315) and validation gave a much smaller +0.055 (95% -0.231 to +0.342). The point estimates are in the hypothesized direction but are imprecise and not independently supported; this does not establish a scientifically meaningful effect of MYC amplification on the USP7/TP53 association. I therefore keep H115 unresolved and close it, while noting that the direct USP7/TP53-wild-type effect itself remains robust in both MYC-positive and MYC-negative subsets elsewhere in the ledger. Overall, the strongest supported findings remain four large within-dataset associations: SMARCA4 loss with stronger SMARCA2 dependency, KEAP1 loss with weaker NFE2L2 dependency, MSI-high with much stronger WRN dependency, and TP53 wild-type status with stronger USP7 dependency. These are supported by repeated direct subsets and independent validation, not causal claims. Negative or unsupported findings include most RTK/MAPK direct effects, MDM2/RPL11 effects of TP53 loss, MSI-high/DHX9, MYC/POLR2A, ARID1A/SMARCA2, and most formal interactions. Next, I will focus on resolving context specificity and unresolved outcomes: analyze the already registered direct KRAS-mutant subset tests and lineage/Cas9 interactions for the four core effects, then test the newly registered MYC, aneuploidy, KEAP1, ERBB2, STAT3, TMED10, RIT1, KIF18A, and DHX9 hypotheses before considering any additional broad marker screening.",
  "research_notes": "Current synthesis state: H158 validated and closed; H186 validated and closed; H115 non-robust interaction unresolved/closed. The four core associations (SMARCA4/SMARCA2, KEAP1/NFE2L2, MSI-high/WRN, TP53-WT/USP7) are robustly supported within this supplied lung dataset and across many screen-quality, library, mutation-burden, co-mutation, and culture contexts. Negative findings remain informative: direct RTK/MAPK effects are generally null, TP53 loss does not associate meaningfully with MDM2 or RPL11 dependency, MSI-high does not associate with DHX9, MYC amplification does not associate with POLR2A, and ARID1A loss is null for SMARCA2. Non-robust effect modification includes the MYC interaction for USP7 and the Ky-library USP7 interaction family. No validation requests remain. Next analysis priority: (1) analyze active direct KRAS-mutant context tests H198-H200 and registered lineage subtype and Cas9-activity interactions; (2) analyze MYC-context tests in MSI-negative cells for WRN and DHX9; (3) analyze newly registered unresolved-outcome proposals for KIF18A, STAT3, TMED10, RIT1, and DHX9, especially MYC and stress-context hypotheses; (4) avoid additional low-prior marker screening unless a targeted test reveals a large unexpected signal. All conclusions remain dataset-specific associations among measured variables, not causal claims or claims of external generalizability."
}

