  "narrative": "The validation response for H61 confirms the low-mutation-burden USP7/TP53 result. H61 compares dependency_USP7 in TP53 wild-type versus TP53-loss lines within lines with mutation_burden <= 5.25, with registered direction -1. Discovery gave a signed estimate of +0.3146 (95% interval +0.2124 to +0.4169), and independent validation gave +0.3152 (adjusted validation interval +0.1648 to +0.4656). Because the exposed cell is TP53 wild-type and the comparator is TP53 loss, the positive signed estimate means TP53 wild-type cells have more negative dependency_USP7 scores, i.e., stronger USP7 dependency, than TP53-loss cells in this low mutation burden context. The discovery and validation point estimates are nearly identical, and the validation interval excludes zero at the stringent validation threshold. The effect is large relative to the outcome scale and is scientifically meaningful in this dataset. I accept H61 and close it.\n\nFinal scientific synthesis. The strongest supported within-dataset associations are four large effects: SMARCA4 loss is associated with markedly stronger SMARCA2 dependency; KEAP1 loss is associated with markedly weaker, less negative NFE2L2 dependency; MSI-high status is associated with markedly stronger WRN dependency; and TP53 wild-type status is associated with stronger USP7 dependency relative to TP53 loss. These associations are supported by overall tests, many repeated direct subset confirmations, and several independent validations, including H61 here for the USP7/TP53-wild-type effect among low mutation burden lines. The core effects are orders of magnitude larger than the marginal null-scale contrasts observed elsewhere and should be treated as the main evidence-backed findings in this dataset.\n\nNegative or unsupported findings include most direct RTK/MAPK and marker hypotheses: KRAS mutation effects on KRAS, BRAF, EGFR, ERBB2, and RIT1 dependencies; BRAF V600E effects on BRAF and RIT1; EGFR amplification effect on EGFR dependency was null/nonmeaningful in validation; ERBB2 amplification effect on ERBB2 dependency; TP53 loss effects on MDM2 and RPL11; MSI-high effects on DHX9; ARID1A loss effect on SMARCA2; MYC amplification effects on MDM2, KIF18A, DHX9, and STAT3 in TP53 wild-type cells; TP53 wild-type effect on STAT3; and most formal interaction hypotheses, including MYC interaction on USP7/TP53 and library interaction claims that did not validate. These negative conclusions mean the evidence does not support a scientifically meaningful association in this dataset, not that the true effect is exactly zero.\n\nUnresolved uncertainty remains mainly for smaller effects and underpowered contexts. H193, TP53-loss versus TP53-wild-type effect on dependency_KIF18A, is statistically directional overall (+0.021, interval +0.001 to +0.040), but the effect is small and close to the practical threshold; I keep it unresolved pending registered context tests. Several formal interactions and subset-specific effects remain active or unresolved because they are either imprecise, underpowered, or not yet tested. The most informative next analysis is to run the already registered context tests for H193 in high aneuploidy and high mutation burden strata, along with a limited set of mechanistically motivated direct context tests such as SMARCA4-loss/SMARCA2 and KEAP1-loss/NFE2L2 in MYC-amplified cells. After those, remaining formal interactions should be analyzed only if cell counts support them; otherwise they should remain unresolved or closed as inconclusive. All conclusions are associations in the supplied lung-cell-line dataset, not causal statements or claims of external generalizability.",
  "research_notes": "No voluntary validation requests remain. H61 accepted and closed after validation: low-mutation-burden USP7 effect for TP53 wild-type versus TP53 loss is reproducible, signed estimate +0.315 in both discovery and validation, validation interval +0.165 to +0.466 excluding zero.\n\nCore accepted robust dataset-specific associations: SMARCA4 loss -> much stronger SMARCA2 dependency; KEAP1 loss -> much weaker/less negative NFE2L2 dependency; MSI-high -> much stronger WRN dependency; TP53 wild-type -> stronger USP7 dependency versus TP53 loss. These have direct subset confirmations and/or independent validation.\n\nNegative/unsupported areas: most RTK/MAPK marker effects, MSI-high/DHX9, TP53-loss/MDM2/RPL11, ARID1A/SMARCA2, MYC/POLR2A, MYC effects in TP53-WT for MDM2/KIF18A/DHX9/STAT3, TP53-WT/STAT3, BRAF/RIT1, MYC interaction for USP7, library interactions.\n\nUnresolved focus: H193 KIF18A/TP53-loss is statistically directional but small (+0.021; CI +0.001 to +0.040); prioritize registered context tests H221 and H222. Also analyze high-priority direct context claims H213/H214 and selected formal interactions if cells support them. Avoid additional low-prior marker screening. All findings are within-dataset associations, not causal or generalizable claims."
