  "narrative": "The new panel accomplishes two different things cleanly. First, it provides strong confirmations of the four main within-dataset associations in the largest default omics-profile group. In RNA+WGS lines, SMARCA4-loss cells show markedly stronger SMARCA2 dependency than SMARCA4-intact lines, H223 gives a signed estimate of +0.778 with a very narrow interval far above zero; KEAP1-loss cells show markedly weaker NFE2L2 dependency, H224 gives +0.796; MSI-high cells show markedly stronger WRN dependency, H225 gives +0.813; and TP53 wild-type cells show stronger USP7 dependency than TP53-loss cells, H226 gives +0.447. These effects are large, precise, and many times larger than the null-scale contrasts seen in many other comparisons. They are also internally consistent with the overall accepted core effects across many technical and biological subsets. I accept H223-H226 and close them as dataset-specific associations, not causal statements.\n\nSecond, the KIF18A/TP53-loss context tests show that the small residual signal from H193 is not sufficiently consistent to be interpreted as a robust biologically or clinically meaningful association. The original H193 estimate is only +0.021, with a lower bound just above zero and relative effect near or below the 10% clinical-importance scale. The new context tests are heterogeneous: H222 in high mutation burden and H230 in low aneuploidy give directionally positive signed estimates of +0.032 and +0.036 with intervals excluding zero, but the lower bounds are small and well below the 10% threshold. Given that these are repeated, exploratory subset tests without familywise error control, I do not think either is strong enough to declare a stratum-specific effect; I leave them unresolved and active because formal interaction tests are a better follow-up than another set of subgroup mean differences. H227 in RNA+WGS gives +0.025 but its interval just crosses zero, and H233 in adenocarcinoma gives +0.022 with an interval crossing zero and a point estimate below the 10%-scale benchmark; these are insufficient to support a meaningful stratum-specific effect, so H227 is unresolved/deferred and H233 is rejected/closed. H221 in high aneuploidy, H228 and H229 by screen ROC quality, and H234 in batch A are clearly interval-crossing and not directionally supported, so I reject and close them as unsupported context claims.\n\nBecause the context panel is inconsistent and the original effect is small relative to the clinical-importance benchmark, I reassess H193 as unresolved and active rather than maintaining it as an accepted scientifically meaningful association. The evidence suggests a possible small directional trend in TP53-loss lines, but it is not precise enough, not consistent enough across strata, and not large enough relative to the dependency-score scale to support a firm conclusion. I registered two formal interaction proposals, testing effect modification by high mutation burden and low aneuploidy, to avoid continuing to chase multiple borderline subset mean differences. Validation requests are exhausted, so no validation is requested. All conclusions remain associations in the supplied lung-cell-line dataset, with biological interpretation and generalizability uncertain.",
  "research_notes": "Latest state after the RNA+WGS and KIF18A context panel.\n\nCore supported within-dataset associations:\n- SMARCA4 loss -> stronger SMARCA2 dependency: accepted overall and across many strata, including RNA+WGS (H223), KIF18A context not relevant.\n- KEAP1 loss -> weaker NFE2L2 dependency: accepted overall and across many strata, including RNA+WGS (H224).\n- MSI-high -> stronger WRN dependency: accepted overall and across many strata, including RNA+WGS (H225).\n- TP53 wild-type -> stronger USP7 dependency vs TP53 loss: accepted overall and across many strata, including RNA+WGS (H226). H61 validation remains confirmed low-TMB USP7/TP53 wild-type effect.\n\nThese are the primary evidence-backed associations in this dataset. They are large, precise, and supported by repeated subset confirmations. They are associations in supplied lung-cell-line data, not causal or external claims.\n\nKIF18A/TP53-loss:\n- Overall H193 was near-threshold small (+0.0208, interval barely excluding zero). The new context panel is inconsistent: high TMB (+0.032, CI excludes zero), low aneuploidy (+0.036, CI excludes zero), RNA+WGS (+0.025, CI just crosses zero), adenocarcinoma (+0.022, CI crosses), while high aneuploidy, screen-quality split, and batch A are not supported.\n- Due to multiplicity, borderline lower bounds, and heterogeneity, I no longer treat H193 as accepted. Status unresolved/active.\n- Registered two formal interactions: TP53-loss effect modified by high mutation burden and by low aneuploidy. These are better than further subset mean differences.\n- Existing low-TMB H231 and high-ploidy H232 remain active as secondary complements but are lower priority than formal interactions if analysis slots are limited.\n\nNo validation budget remains. Future analyses should focus on resolving KIF18A only if formal interactions are clearly supportive; otherwise close as inconclusive/exploratory. Remaining broad screening, marker, and weak interaction claims should be deferred or closed unless they serve a direct prioritized question."
